WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
I can lie to myself and say,

00:00:03.159 --> 00:00:04.800
oh, well I need to do these races

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:05.920
that everybody tells me to do,

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.679
or I can just follow what I'm passionate about.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:09.000
And to me it was,

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.079
I thought that I would probably have more success

00:00:11.079 --> 00:00:13.239
if I followed what I actually wanted to do.

00:00:14.079 --> 00:00:15.599
And so that's what I did.

00:00:16.199 --> 00:00:17.280
And it worked out, I mean.

00:00:17.519 --> 00:00:19.039
It worked out great, I guess.

00:00:21.559 --> 00:00:23.519
Well, thanks for coming to

00:00:23.519 --> 00:00:24.719
chat with me. It's a pleasure.

00:00:26.079 --> 00:00:29.320
Your last season has been like...

00:00:30.559 --> 00:00:34.079
just something out of this world.

00:00:35.039 --> 00:00:38.800
It's been great to see your progress.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.960
Yeah. Very fast.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:45.480
But also how you have been able to...

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.320
to jump from different distances, I think.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:49.960
That's the hardest thing, as well.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:53.760
You have been very competitive in short ultras

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.840
and very long ultras and coming back to that.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:57.320
And that's very rare.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:58.500
Yeah.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.559
And showing... It reminds me a bit like...

00:01:02.559 --> 00:01:05.320
You know, Lynn Hill, the climber,

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:10.400
who was the first person to free-climb El Capitan.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:12.880
And was, "Here it goes, boys."

00:01:13.119 --> 00:01:15.239
And I think you are our

00:01:15.239 --> 00:01:16.320
Lynn Hill in ultra-running.

00:01:16.639 --> 00:01:18.079
Oh, my God. That is really like...

00:01:18.079 --> 00:01:18.840
Thank you. I'm using that.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:22.880
And, yeah, thanks for bringing all this

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:24.559
inspiration to our women

00:01:24.639 --> 00:01:26.639
and all trail runners.

00:01:27.639 --> 00:01:29.440
So, yeah, thanks for that.

00:01:29.719 --> 00:01:30.679
Yeah, not a problem. I mean,

00:01:30.679 --> 00:01:31.480
I'm just out there running.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:34.199
So, yeah, it's not a problem for me.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:35.599
Yeah, no, but I think it's...

00:01:36.079 --> 00:01:38.320
Probably it's that, like, I think that we...

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:41.320
I don't know how you feel, but it's just like...

00:01:42.119 --> 00:01:43.800
that we love running and I think...

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.679
The love for what we do, it brings

00:01:47.679 --> 00:01:49.800
us to the places where we end up.

00:01:50.239 --> 00:01:52.159
Yeah. I definitely agree there,

00:01:52.159 --> 00:01:55.039
because I've been doing Cocodona now for...

00:01:55.039 --> 00:01:56.840
This was my third year doing it,

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:58.760
and I have always felt like

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:01.440
it was a really important race.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.480
Like, the visibility that Jamil gives that race

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:06.840
and the attention that he

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.239
puts on that race just...

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:10.360
I don't know, feels very special.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:11.920
And so I've always thought that...

00:02:13.199 --> 00:02:14.159
I haven't necessarily

00:02:14.159 --> 00:02:15.880
always been met with enthusiasm

00:02:16.079 --> 00:02:17.119
when I tell people, like,

00:02:17.119 --> 00:02:18.079
"Oh, I'm doing Cocodona."

00:02:18.079 --> 00:02:21.039
They're like, "Well, why? Why don't you do UTMB?"

00:02:21.039 --> 00:02:22.320
Or, "Why don't you do Western States?"

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:23.280
Or, "Why don't you do anything

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.639
else that's more competitive?"

00:02:26.079 --> 00:02:26.760
Yeah, right, yeah.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:27.719
But it's like, well, I...

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:30.400
Because this feels important to me.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:32.000
And so over the last year,

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:33.760
it's been really fun to...

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:38.239
I don't want to say be proven right, but I knew

00:02:38.239 --> 00:02:39.239
there was something here.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:41.679
And then something happened.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:43.880
And so it's just like, "Ha-ha."

00:02:44.239 --> 00:02:44.739
Yeah, yeah.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:47.440
It's an awesome... Yeah, it's an awesome race.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:48.880
And it's a hard thing to do, 

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:51.119
I think, like, as you are a...

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.760
young athlete growing into the sport and saying,

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:59.760
"OK, you have the talent to win all the races.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.119
Like, you can go to Western

00:03:01.119 --> 00:03:03.599
States, to UTMB and be there."

00:03:03.719 --> 00:03:05.719
We'll see. And, yeah, you have the talent.

00:03:05.719 --> 00:03:06.800
You have been proving that, like...

00:03:07.639 --> 00:03:09.360
I don't think about Cocodona,

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:10.760
but all the other shorter races.

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:12.639
You prove that you have that talent.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.960
But to say, like, "No, I want to do this,"

00:03:16.639 --> 00:03:19.800
because probably gives you something that you

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:20.760
know that you will not find

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:22.599
in other races.

00:03:23.639 --> 00:03:26.440
But, like, the industry is

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:28.000
really going much more, like,

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.719
onto, like, very straight lines. Yeah.

00:03:30.719 --> 00:03:31.800
So, like, you do that,

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:34.199
like, you do the 50 miles, 50k,

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:36.840
and then it's like, Western, UTMB...

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:39.239
I'm not an example, because the last years, I

00:03:39.239 --> 00:03:40.239
have only been with the races.

00:03:40.239 --> 00:03:42.519
But... You've done plenty of things

00:03:42.519 --> 00:03:44.519
that have not been those races. No, no.

00:03:44.519 --> 00:03:46.280
But, I mean, it's very, like, on those lines.

00:03:46.519 --> 00:03:48.920
You do, like, a short race, and then it's, like,

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:50.159
the golden and that and that.

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:53.440
And you have proved that, yeah, there are other ways

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:56.639
to develop yourself as a runner 

00:03:56.719 --> 00:03:59.159
and not following, like,

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:02.320
the trends of the industry,

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:03.239
but finding your way.

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:06.880
And, yeah, how did it come about for you?

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:08.599
Like, on that, like, it

00:04:08.599 --> 00:04:10.400
was like a conscious choice,

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:11.760
or it was more like you

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.199
were, like, it ended up there.

00:04:14.199 --> 00:04:16.639
Well, you know, I have only

00:04:16.639 --> 00:04:17.800
been a pro now for the past...

00:04:19.119 --> 00:04:19.760
not even year.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.199
And so, for me, it was most of

00:04:22.199 --> 00:04:23.880
an issue of, like, resources.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:25.119
Like, I just, you know, I was

00:04:25.119 --> 00:04:26.519
working full-time in healthcare.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:29.719
I didn't have the money to do a lot of the races

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:31.760
that I was supposed to be doing.

00:04:32.239 --> 00:04:34.000
And so I just made the best with what I could.

00:04:34.639 --> 00:04:37.880
And it was really frustrating to feel like I...

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:40.599
I felt like I was good at the sport,

00:04:40.599 --> 00:04:42.320
but I wasn't being taken seriously.

00:04:42.599 --> 00:04:44.360
Because you're not doing those races. Right.

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:45.039
And it's like, well, no

00:04:45.039 --> 00:04:46.239
one cares about this race.

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:48.159
Like, you need to sign up for this race.

00:04:48.159 --> 00:04:48.679
And it's like, well, I

00:04:48.679 --> 00:04:49.800
can't sign up for that race,

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:51.960
because that race requires international travel,

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:53.599
and I have two weeks of vacation.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:55.400
And so, you know, it just...

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:58.079
It did feel like I was getting kind of stifled.

00:05:00.079 --> 00:05:01.079
But, yeah, and then I don't know.

00:05:01.119 --> 00:05:01.880
I mean, I...

00:05:03.679 --> 00:05:04.760
Kind of, you know, that

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:06.360
was like a four-year thing

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:08.199
of me feeling like I was really...

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:10.519
I don't know, not necessarily

00:05:10.519 --> 00:05:12.199
the best, but I was certainly good.

00:05:12.199 --> 00:05:13.000
Like, I was winning the

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:14.239
races that I signed up for,

00:05:14.239 --> 00:05:15.800
and that had to mean something.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.880
And, yeah, and so I kind

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:21.599
of fell into Cocodona,

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:22.960
because I'd been watching

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:24.440
the livestream for years.

00:05:25.199 --> 00:05:27.679
And I just loved the production.

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:29.400
And I loved the story. That's generous, yeah.

00:05:29.519 --> 00:05:31.079
Because it's a long race to follow, yeah.

00:05:31.079 --> 00:05:34.079
It's very long, but, you know, I loved all of it.

00:05:34.079 --> 00:05:36.800
And I loved the equality that

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:38.320
they gave every participant.

00:05:38.639 --> 00:05:39.679
Like, every participant from

00:05:39.679 --> 00:05:41.079
the first finisher to the last one

00:05:41.079 --> 00:05:42.599
gets an opportunity to be on that livestream.

00:05:42.599 --> 00:05:44.039
And I loved that story.

00:05:44.639 --> 00:05:46.400
Because I do think that within Ultra Running,

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:49.440
you have a unique opportunity, I think,

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:53.039
to both marvel at the athleticism of people

00:05:53.039 --> 00:05:55.519
and also be intrigued by

00:05:55.519 --> 00:05:56.760
the stories that they can tell

00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:58.039
through their athletics.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:00.519
And Cocodona was this beautiful marriage

00:06:00.519 --> 00:06:01.679
of both of those things.

00:06:02.199 --> 00:06:05.079
And so, yeah, I just was enthralled by the race

00:06:05.079 --> 00:06:07.360
and then signed up, and that became like,

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:09.960
"Well, I think this is really cool,

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:11.519
and that everybody tells me to do,

00:06:11.519 --> 00:06:13.320
or I can just follow what I'm passionate about."

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:14.639
And to me, it was...

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:16.679
I thought that I would probably have more success

00:06:16.679 --> 00:06:18.920
if I followed what I actually wanted to do.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:21.199
And so that's what I did.

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:22.840
And it worked out, I mean.

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:24.679
Yeah, yeah, yeah, absolutely.
It worked out great, I guess.

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:27.159
And I think it's something also very special

00:06:27.159 --> 00:06:28.119
in these long...

00:06:29.599 --> 00:06:31.400
like, very long distance events,

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:32.480
but it's very different, I think.

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:35.599
100 miles, you feel it's a race.

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:38.079
Yeah. And you are racing all the time.

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:38.820
Yeah.

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:42.719
And while in these longer events,

00:06:43.039 --> 00:06:47.119
you have some moments where you are fighting

00:06:47.199 --> 00:06:51.079
like inside you, like you are not racing anymore,

00:06:51.079 --> 00:06:53.079
but you are really facing...

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:55.960
you are putting a mirror in front of you,

00:06:55.960 --> 00:06:58.320
and you are facing all these things.

00:06:59.239 --> 00:07:01.079
And I think that's the most interesting

00:07:01.079 --> 00:07:03.119
of these very long distance.

00:07:04.159 --> 00:07:06.920
And how did it feel for you?

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:08.599
Like, what was the experience?

00:07:09.519 --> 00:07:11.079
Because it's hard for people to say...

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:13.440
it's very hard physically.

00:07:13.719 --> 00:07:15.360
Like, you have pain everywhere in the body.

00:07:15.679 --> 00:07:16.599
Yeah, sometimes.

00:07:17.239 --> 00:07:19.920
Yeah, yeah, but it brings you some things

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:21.800
that you can't find elsewhere.

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:25.280
I think for me, the thing that I've learned

00:07:25.599 --> 00:07:28.559
from doing those multi-day events is...

00:07:30.159 --> 00:07:33.760
how everything can be overcome, I think.

00:07:34.159 --> 00:07:36.119
Because all you have is

00:07:36.119 --> 00:07:37.679
time when you're out there.

00:07:37.679 --> 00:07:38.320
And, you know, if you...

00:07:38.559 --> 00:07:40.079
I remember the first time I did Cocadona,

00:07:40.079 --> 00:07:43.599
it was really intimidating to start the race

00:07:43.679 --> 00:07:46.679
because you know that you have 250 miles

00:07:47.199 --> 00:07:48.360
until you get to be done.

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:49.860
And it just...

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:51.800
I don't know, that's very intimidating.

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:52.440
And so...

00:07:54.719 --> 00:07:56.159
I learned a lot that first year.

00:07:56.159 --> 00:07:57.119
I was really overwhelmed...

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:00.320
I think for the first half of the race.

00:08:01.039 --> 00:08:02.239
And at the halfway point,

00:08:02.239 --> 00:08:04.320
I actually had a really low moment

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:06.400
where I was like, "Oh my gosh, I feel terrible."

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:08.719
And I'm only halfway there.

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:11.400
And like, I don't understand how I'm going to...

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:13.719
I knew that I was going to make it to the finish

00:08:13.719 --> 00:08:16.039
because that was just not negotiable.

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:18.319
But I didn't know how I was going to do it.

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:19.560
You find your ways.

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:21.199
You find... You figure it out.

00:08:21.319 --> 00:08:22.800
You really figure it out.

00:08:23.079 --> 00:08:25.560
If that's what you want to do, then it happens.

00:08:27.199 --> 00:08:27.800
But yeah, and then this...

00:08:28.039 --> 00:08:29.079
And so I learned a lot of things

00:08:29.079 --> 00:08:31.159
in that second half about like...

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:34.039
Without being too philosophical.

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:37.720
Like, time is not what it seems.

00:08:38.079 --> 00:08:38.800
Yeah, time is...

00:08:39.159 --> 00:08:40.039
Well, like, that's...

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:42.279
We know that time and space are like...

00:08:42.479 --> 00:08:46.039
If you take quantum physics, it's flexible.

00:08:46.399 --> 00:08:46.899
Right.

00:08:47.159 --> 00:08:47.960
And that's something that...

00:08:48.279 --> 00:08:49.079
Yeah, I don't know how...

00:08:49.119 --> 00:08:50.680
I have experienced that on

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:52.159
these long projects that...

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:53.560
Sometimes you can be...

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:56.760
Like, I'm looking at the watch and it's like,

00:08:57.119 --> 00:08:58.359
"Okay, it's three in the

00:08:58.359 --> 00:09:00.680
morning and five minutes after,

00:09:01.199 --> 00:09:04.439
you look and it's like, "Wow, it's like three in

00:09:04.439 --> 00:09:05.319
the morning the next day."

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:06.800
And you have the impression

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:08.239
that it's been only five minutes.

00:09:08.239 --> 00:09:08.739
Yeah.

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:11.880
And I don't ever really look at...

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:14.119
Two out of three Cocodonas

00:09:14.119 --> 00:09:15.199
I've done without a watch.

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:18.640
So I don't care now what time it is

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:20.479
or what happens, it's just like you go...

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:21.840
With something like Cocodona

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:23.119
or even something like this...

00:09:23.119 --> 00:09:23.619
To keep moving.

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:24.560
It's just like keep...

00:09:25.359 --> 00:09:27.119
You know, it doesn't matter.

00:09:27.359 --> 00:09:28.960
Your objective is to get to the finish.

00:09:29.239 --> 00:09:30.279
And it doesn't matter how

00:09:30.279 --> 00:09:31.199
long it takes you to get there.

00:09:31.399 --> 00:09:32.520
Like, just keep trying as

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:33.640
hard as you can to get there.

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:34.640
And if...

00:09:35.479 --> 00:09:38.079
You know, barring some catastrophe, you know,

00:09:38.079 --> 00:09:39.520
it will probably happen for you.

00:09:40.199 --> 00:09:40.720
And I just...

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:41.720
You know, there have been a

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:43.119
lot of instances over the past,

00:09:43.119 --> 00:09:43.880
I would say, year that

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:45.079
I've noticed where people have

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:49.159
very heroically decided to continue.

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:51.880
And I do think Molly Seidel at Western States,

00:09:52.159 --> 00:09:52.840
like she...

00:09:53.399 --> 00:09:54.520
And I loved you guys'

00:09:54.520 --> 00:09:55.800
conversation where you talked about

00:09:56.159 --> 00:09:58.000
where winning almost feels like a relief.

00:09:59.039 --> 00:10:00.520
And so what happens when you

00:10:00.520 --> 00:10:01.640
know you're not gonna get that?

00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:03.000
Like, what motivates you to continue?

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:05.880
And I was pacing a runner

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:07.640
named Devin Yanko earlier this year

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:08.640
at Scout Mountain.

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:11.239
And she is somebody who has very high

00:10:11.239 --> 00:10:12.279
expectations for herself

00:10:12.479 --> 00:10:13.439
that she feels is

00:10:13.439 --> 00:10:15.119
reinforced by external pressures.

00:10:15.439 --> 00:10:16.840
Like, she feels like she has to win.

00:10:17.279 --> 00:10:18.399
And if she doesn't, then,

00:10:18.439 --> 00:10:20.439
like, she's disappointing people.

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:23.119
And it was incredible to get

00:10:23.119 --> 00:10:24.920
to firsthand watch her, like,

00:10:27.199 --> 00:10:28.319
decide to continue anyway.

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:30.560
And, like, she didn't care about place.

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:31.840
She didn't care about time.

00:10:32.079 --> 00:10:33.319
All she cared about was, like...

00:10:34.199 --> 00:10:36.000
We chatted a little bit about Adam Peterman.

00:10:36.439 --> 00:10:37.760
Yeah, liking Western.

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:39.640
Yeah, and, like, she said, you know,

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:41.560
I always told myself that I would

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:42.880
be the type of person to continue

00:10:43.199 --> 00:10:44.520
if nothing was wrong.

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:46.239
If I just wasn't having it any other day,

00:10:46.239 --> 00:10:47.840
I would be the kind of person who would continue.

00:10:48.319 --> 00:10:49.359
And now I get an opportunity

00:10:49.359 --> 00:10:51.079
to, like, see if that's true.

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:52.760
And I just think that's...

00:10:53.399 --> 00:10:54.960
Oh, cool. Yeah, that's so cool.

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:56.760
And so, I mean, yeah, for me, it's like...

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:00.000
Again, barring any major catastrophe, like,

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:00.640
I don't have any

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:03.319
expectations going into UTMB, you know?

00:11:03.319 --> 00:11:04.960
I just want to have a good performance.

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:07.760
And I want to make it back.

00:11:08.479 --> 00:11:10.920
Yeah, and I think to experience,

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:14.640
like, sometimes when we are racing,

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:15.520
competing, like, it's...

00:11:16.159 --> 00:11:19.720
We are so focused on, like, what's the outcome?

00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:22.199
That we... The outcome,

00:11:22.199 --> 00:11:24.039
it blurs the experience.

00:11:24.319 --> 00:11:24.819
Yeah.

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:27.000
And I think that's the fun thing, is, like, it's

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:28.239
an amazing experience, like,

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:30.479
to run around those mountains,

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:32.039
to spend the night out there,

00:11:32.039 --> 00:11:35.199
like, to share with all the people that is here,

00:11:35.199 --> 00:11:36.079
with all the other runners,

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:38.079
with ourselves, like, the body,

00:11:38.079 --> 00:11:39.520
what we experience, and that is,

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:41.640
like, enjoy every second of that,

00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:43.239
because it's... If you are just

00:11:43.239 --> 00:11:44.520
thinking about the outcome, like...

00:11:46.119 --> 00:11:48.800
You might be disappointed, or you might be happy,

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:52.520
but it will be just, like, a second of emotion,

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:53.920
while, like, at experience,

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:55.520
it will last for very long.

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:58.880
Yeah, I had a... Back in, I think it was 2022,

00:12:00.159 --> 00:12:02.520
I, you know, I'd had some success leading up to

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:03.880
then at, like, again, local races.

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:07.199
And I did have a season, I would say,

00:12:07.279 --> 00:12:08.840
where I thought that winning

00:12:08.840 --> 00:12:10.000
was the most important thing,

00:12:10.319 --> 00:12:13.920
and very quickly I learned that that attitude

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:15.000
does not get you anywhere,

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:18.479
because I was doing Cascade Crest, and I was

00:12:18.479 --> 00:12:20.359
over-trained going into it,

00:12:20.359 --> 00:12:21.760
because I was trying so hard

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:23.680
to win that I trained too much,

00:12:24.199 --> 00:12:25.800
and so I got to the race

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:28.960
exhausted, and then everything hurt,

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:31.560
and so I took too much ibuprofen and, like, wound

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:32.640
up with a stomach bleed,

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:33.920
and, like, I still finished the

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:36.119
race, but, like, it was just, like,

00:12:36.119 --> 00:12:38.880
"Okay, I need to do some work. I need to do some

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:40.119
work on this attitude thing,"

00:12:40.119 --> 00:12:42.319
because if that... I can't

00:12:42.319 --> 00:12:43.960
have this happen again, you know?

00:12:44.319 --> 00:12:46.199
And I think if winning is your

00:12:46.199 --> 00:12:47.720
only objective that you care about,

00:12:47.720 --> 00:12:49.680
there's only one winner of any race ever.

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:50.260
Exactly.

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:53.279
And, like, chances are easily that it could be...

00:12:53.279 --> 00:12:55.359
Yeah, the chances are that you'll be disappointed.

00:12:55.359 --> 00:12:57.439
Exactly, exactly. So you might as well figure out

00:12:57.439 --> 00:12:59.079
what your other goals are.

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:00.760
And that's cool, yeah.

00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:04.479
And when... Yeah, you mentioned that, like,

00:13:04.479 --> 00:13:06.439
did it change only, like, mentality or also,

00:13:06.439 --> 00:13:07.640
like, training approach?

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:08.500
Like...

00:13:09.239 --> 00:13:12.319
Um, I think, for me, I don't know if it changed

00:13:12.319 --> 00:13:13.840
my training mentality that much,

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:16.199
because I still... I train a lot, and I, like...

00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:17.520
I don't know, I'm high...

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:18.079
How did you do that? Like,

00:13:18.079 --> 00:13:19.800
being, like, working full-time?

00:13:20.079 --> 00:13:21.399
I didn't sleep very much.

00:13:21.399 --> 00:13:21.899
Yeah.

00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:23.000
But...

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:24.479
You don't need to... It's like, you feel like you

00:13:24.479 --> 00:13:25.199
don't need to sleep very much?

00:13:25.199 --> 00:13:27.800
No, I don't think so. Which is good and bad.

00:13:28.399 --> 00:13:30.079
No, I think it's good, yeah. I think it's good.

00:13:30.079 --> 00:13:30.560
I think it's good.

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:31.399
And he's like, "Yeah."

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:33.199
Yeah, I noticed during your

00:13:33.199 --> 00:13:35.159
states of elevation, it was like,

00:13:35.159 --> 00:13:36.479
"Yeah, you average, like, four

00:13:36.479 --> 00:13:37.800
hours of sleep, and I get that."

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:38.520
Yeah, yeah.
Like, I'm pretty

00:13:38.520 --> 00:13:40.079
functional off little sleep, too.

00:13:40.359 --> 00:13:42.680
But, um... But, yeah, I was mostly not sleeping.

00:13:43.520 --> 00:13:44.359
But I think the biggest thing

00:13:44.359 --> 00:13:45.680
for me has been the shift in

00:13:46.319 --> 00:13:48.159
what I need to think is

00:13:48.159 --> 00:13:49.960
important when I step up to a start line.

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:53.119
Because, yeah, I was... I grew up playing sports,

00:13:53.119 --> 00:13:55.079
and while I wasn't necessarily going to win them,

00:13:56.359 --> 00:13:57.319
competition and, like,

00:13:57.319 --> 00:13:58.960
doing your best, or maybe...

00:13:59.520 --> 00:14:01.119
I don't know, I think I confused

00:14:01.119 --> 00:14:03.199
doing your best with being the best.

00:14:03.760 --> 00:14:08.479
And so I had to figure out the difference and

00:14:08.479 --> 00:14:10.159
then apply that to running.

00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:11.199
Because, yeah, I thought

00:14:11.199 --> 00:14:13.039
that, like, if you didn't win,

00:14:13.039 --> 00:14:13.640
then you were just going

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:15.720
to, like, disappoint people.

00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:17.800
Yeah. I think that's also very cool, too.

00:14:17.800 --> 00:14:20.720
Like, I feel that in the US, it's a lot about the

00:14:20.720 --> 00:14:22.520
competition side of, like, yeah.

00:14:23.199 --> 00:14:24.199
Being on the rung.

00:14:24.199 --> 00:14:24.800
Yeah, probably.

00:14:25.439 --> 00:14:27.319
Yeah. It's, uh...

00:14:27.640 --> 00:14:31.199
Yeah. And, I mean, too, it's easy to tell a story

00:14:31.199 --> 00:14:32.239
about somebody who wins.

00:14:32.439 --> 00:14:34.680
Yeah. But we get the

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:35.920
attention, so then you tell a story,

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:37.239
then you get inspired by those

00:14:37.239 --> 00:14:38.560
people, so you want to do the same.

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:40.159
Right. But, yeah, I mean,

00:14:40.159 --> 00:14:41.520
there can only ever be one.

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:44.199
So I think if that's all you're going after,

00:14:44.199 --> 00:14:45.600
then you're probably going to be disappointed.

00:14:46.079 --> 00:14:50.319
Yeah. And why do you run?

00:14:50.720 --> 00:14:51.560
Why do I run? Yeah.

00:14:52.159 --> 00:14:52.960
This is a good question.

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:58.199
So, my friend Stephen Palmer is a thru-hiker,

00:14:59.439 --> 00:15:02.079
and he and I have been friends for over a decade.

00:15:02.760 --> 00:15:05.600
And he's just this, like, fascinating person.

00:15:05.960 --> 00:15:08.399
He's so, like, easygoing and

00:15:08.399 --> 00:15:10.560
patient and, like, whatever.

00:15:11.079 --> 00:15:12.279
And I remember he and I began

00:15:12.279 --> 00:15:15.199
running, and I was very intense about it.

00:15:15.199 --> 00:15:17.520
Like, I was very much, like, "Why

00:15:17.520 --> 00:15:19.000
are we stopping to look at this view?

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:20.880
Like, we're supposed to be running. Like, I don't

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:22.039
want to stop. We have to keep going."

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:24.640
And so he taught me a lot about running.

00:15:24.640 --> 00:15:27.880
And one of the things that he taught me was to

00:15:27.880 --> 00:15:30.520
slow down and, like, be where you are.

00:15:31.119 --> 00:15:32.800
And then the other thing that he taught me was

00:15:32.800 --> 00:15:34.000
the answer to that question,

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:35.520
which is, "Why do you run?" And he

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:37.000
runs because his life is too easy.

00:15:38.079 --> 00:15:40.319
And I think I agree with

00:15:40.319 --> 00:15:41.560
that, and I think I do the same.

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:43.760
You know, I'm really, really lucky.

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:47.199
I was born without any problems.

00:15:48.399 --> 00:15:49.560
My life is pretty easy.

00:15:50.199 --> 00:15:51.479
We take for granted a lot of things.

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:55.239
Yes. And I really think that if you don't

00:15:55.239 --> 00:15:59.359
practice skills that, you know,

00:15:59.560 --> 00:16:00.760
for when things are difficult,

00:16:01.119 --> 00:16:03.319
then when those things happen in life

00:16:03.319 --> 00:16:05.439
that you can't control, then you fall apart.

00:16:06.319 --> 00:16:09.680
And running is a constant practice in coping

00:16:09.680 --> 00:16:11.319
mechanisms for when things are difficult.

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:14.199
And I found that in my personal life, I can

00:16:14.199 --> 00:16:15.600
usually apply those same skills

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:18.319
of just, like, be patient, wait 20 minutes.

00:16:18.880 --> 00:16:20.119
It's probably not as big of

00:16:20.119 --> 00:16:21.720
a deal as it seems right now.

00:16:21.720 --> 00:16:23.600
Just, like, just take a second

00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:24.760
and, like, look at the facts.

00:16:25.119 --> 00:16:26.119
Like, those are all things

00:16:26.119 --> 00:16:27.439
that I learned while running

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:30.199
that I can apply to, like, work stress or

00:16:30.199 --> 00:16:31.960
relationship stress or those kinds of things.

00:16:32.560 --> 00:16:34.479
Because, yeah, I just, you know, I'm very lucky.

00:16:34.479 --> 00:16:38.960
I don't have a lot of external issues, I guess,

00:16:38.960 --> 00:16:40.960
in my life that are difficult.

00:16:41.840 --> 00:16:43.319
But that doesn't mean that I don't want to be

00:16:43.319 --> 00:16:44.319
prepared for when they happen.

00:16:44.800 --> 00:16:47.479
Yeah. And running teaches me how to be prepared.

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:50.560
Yeah, it teaches you to be resilient and to have

00:16:50.560 --> 00:16:54.239
tools for when things happen.

00:16:54.479 --> 00:16:56.560
Yeah. So, yeah, I run because my life's too easy.

00:16:57.279 --> 00:16:58.880
Yeah, that's no, but that's a beautiful answer.

00:16:59.239 --> 00:17:00.439
That's a beautiful answer on that.

00:17:01.479 --> 00:17:04.520
And how do you feel, like, you mentioned, like,

00:17:04.520 --> 00:17:06.359
you have been pro, like, for one year,

00:17:06.359 --> 00:17:07.439
like, less than one year now.

00:17:07.439 --> 00:17:07.939
Yeah.

00:17:08.960 --> 00:17:10.439
How... And I think that's

00:17:10.439 --> 00:17:12.880
a hard thing to become pro

00:17:12.880 --> 00:17:14.319
and not feel the stress of,

00:17:14.319 --> 00:17:15.560
like, wanting to do too much.

00:17:15.600 --> 00:17:18.239
And to say yes to everything

00:17:18.239 --> 00:17:19.720
and all these kinds of things.

00:17:19.720 --> 00:17:20.199
Yeah.

00:17:20.199 --> 00:17:23.720
How did you... For me, it took a lot of years,

00:17:23.720 --> 00:17:26.359
like, to figure out, like, OK, I'm on balance.

00:17:26.760 --> 00:17:27.260
Mm-hmm.

00:17:27.319 --> 00:17:29.199
How do you find this transition from, like,

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:31.840
working full-time and running as, like,

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:34.039
is what you love to do?

00:17:34.720 --> 00:17:38.359
To be doing what you love to do,

00:17:38.359 --> 00:17:39.880
but at the same time, now is...

00:17:40.079 --> 00:17:40.640
A job.

00:17:40.640 --> 00:17:41.279
A job, yeah.

00:17:41.279 --> 00:17:46.279
It was really hard to even consider the idea that

00:17:46.279 --> 00:17:47.640
I could become a professional athlete

00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:50.800
because I do love running so much that I didn't

00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:51.800
want it to feel like work.

00:17:52.560 --> 00:17:53.640
Mm-hmm.
And I'm really glad that...

00:17:54.520 --> 00:17:56.039
Because, you know, I think since the first

00:17:56.039 --> 00:17:58.039
Cocodona, I think I was maybe a little naive

00:17:58.039 --> 00:17:59.439
in thinking that that was enough

00:17:59.439 --> 00:18:01.039
to get me some sort of contract

00:18:01.039 --> 00:18:03.880
and to almost, like, expect something to happen.

00:18:04.479 --> 00:18:06.920
And so through years of, like, not really getting

00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:08.359
what I wanted, I just kind of...

00:18:09.399 --> 00:18:09.920
I don't know.

00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:12.399
I came to this understanding that, like, if I

00:18:12.399 --> 00:18:14.560
ever get the opportunity to, like, turn pro,

00:18:15.640 --> 00:18:17.880
then I will never look at

00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:20.119
running as the job part.

00:18:20.119 --> 00:18:23.319
For me, it's been, like, the interviews and the,

00:18:23.319 --> 00:18:25.039
like, things that I'm

00:18:25.039 --> 00:18:26.079
required to do for sponsors.

00:18:26.399 --> 00:18:27.279
That's the job.
And that's the job.

00:18:27.760 --> 00:18:28.920
But running is not the job.

00:18:29.359 --> 00:18:30.720
Yeah, running is not the job.

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:33.239
But, yeah, I don't know.

00:18:33.239 --> 00:18:34.039
I mean, it's been...

00:18:35.039 --> 00:18:36.520
I think it's been amazing.

00:18:36.560 --> 00:18:39.039
I mean, I've gotten to spend

00:18:39.039 --> 00:18:42.520
six weeks in paradise, kind of.

00:18:43.560 --> 00:18:44.640
So, I mean, I don't know.

00:18:44.640 --> 00:18:45.140
I was...

00:18:45.159 --> 00:18:46.680
As soon as I got approached with,

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:48.279
like, all these offers and stuff,

00:18:48.279 --> 00:18:51.720
I was just, like, immediately pretty pumped to

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:53.319
get to say yes and then get to...

00:18:54.079 --> 00:18:55.720
I mean, it's really hard to

00:18:55.720 --> 00:18:57.880
try to perform at an elite level

00:18:57.880 --> 00:19:00.439
and still hold down an intense job.

00:19:00.439 --> 00:19:00.880
Yeah.

00:19:00.880 --> 00:19:01.760
Yeah, it's harder and harder.

00:19:01.760 --> 00:19:07.000
Like, the depth of field is getting harder.

00:19:07.279 --> 00:19:07.779
Yeah.

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:08.520
So, I mean, I always...

00:19:08.800 --> 00:19:10.640
I think about, like, Lia Yingling and Rachel

00:19:10.640 --> 00:19:11.960
Drake, who still work in,

00:19:11.960 --> 00:19:14.000
like, these intense jobs.

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:15.319
And I'm like, "I don't know how..."

00:19:15.359 --> 00:19:16.520
On top of being parents.

00:19:16.880 --> 00:19:17.279
Yeah.

00:19:17.279 --> 00:19:18.399
It's like, "I don't know how you do it."

00:19:18.399 --> 00:19:22.079
I was drowning half the time and not sleeping.

00:19:23.520 --> 00:19:24.560
So, yeah, I don't know.

00:19:24.560 --> 00:19:25.279
But it's been...

00:19:26.279 --> 00:19:27.960
I mean, it is a dream come true

00:19:27.960 --> 00:19:30.640
for sure, to get to, like, travel.

00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:33.680
And not have to worry.

00:19:33.680 --> 00:19:35.800
Yeah, to have the resources
to do all these things.

00:19:36.239 --> 00:19:36.739
Yeah.

00:19:37.119 --> 00:19:40.399
And I think we are living an amazing moment on

00:19:40.399 --> 00:19:41.520
trail running where, like,

00:19:41.800 --> 00:19:43.640
seeing, like, the performances

00:19:43.640 --> 00:19:46.199
of, like, Courtney in the past years.

00:19:46.199 --> 00:19:46.699
Yes.

00:19:46.880 --> 00:19:48.920
Katie, you...

00:19:49.640 --> 00:19:53.760
It's really, like, Ruth, through the last years,

00:19:53.760 --> 00:19:55.279
like, it's been, like, amazing how they...

00:19:56.239 --> 00:20:01.640
You are closing the gap to the men a lot.

00:20:02.439 --> 00:20:03.920
And it's...

00:20:04.760 --> 00:20:06.520
Like, we live in a society that it's...

00:20:08.119 --> 00:20:11.319
It's very male-oriented on all the levels.

00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:12.100
Yeah.

00:20:13.159 --> 00:20:14.079
And I think it's...

00:20:15.600 --> 00:20:17.640
It's great to see that, like, to show that...

00:20:19.319 --> 00:20:22.279
Yeah, in these things, there's not any

00:20:22.279 --> 00:20:23.279
difference, like, on the races.

00:20:23.520 --> 00:20:25.960
And that the...

00:20:27.640 --> 00:20:28.140
Yeah.

00:20:29.199 --> 00:20:33.920
To break all these glasses and all these mirrors

00:20:33.920 --> 00:20:35.800
that they are to the society.

00:20:36.039 --> 00:20:37.039
And trail running is the same.

00:20:37.359 --> 00:20:39.279
Like, we are a community that

00:20:39.279 --> 00:20:40.640
I think it's pretty welcoming.

00:20:41.079 --> 00:20:44.520
But we are not much different

00:20:44.520 --> 00:20:45.960
than other parts of society.

00:20:46.399 --> 00:20:47.880
Yeah. And it is still very much

00:20:47.880 --> 00:20:50.399
assumed that a male will win anything.

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:53.680
Because I remember, you know, I think I was

00:20:53.680 --> 00:20:56.079
actually in France a couple weeks after

00:20:56.079 --> 00:20:57.920
"Cocadona" and I was jet lagged.

00:20:58.520 --> 00:21:00.359
And so I was like, "Oh, I'm gonna go... I'm gonna

00:21:00.359 --> 00:21:03.000
watch the livestream of "Cocadona" and just see

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:04.279
what was happening in the chat."

00:21:05.399 --> 00:21:07.640
And it was, like, every other comment was

00:21:07.640 --> 00:21:10.199
something about, like, "Oh, she's gonna blow up."

00:21:10.199 --> 00:21:11.439
And, like, "Killian's gonna win."

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:13.079
Right. And, like, it's just...

00:21:14.039 --> 00:21:16.840
I don't know. I think that, particularly with

00:21:16.840 --> 00:21:19.279
endurance, like, ultra-endurance racing,

00:21:19.279 --> 00:21:23.439
I think women have different skills that make us

00:21:23.439 --> 00:21:27.760
equally as talented, maybe.

00:21:27.960 --> 00:21:30.640
Yeah, yeah. The difference is... I think mostly

00:21:30.640 --> 00:21:32.760
in very short terms, like, it's the testosterone.

00:21:33.279 --> 00:21:34.520
It's a big difference, but

00:21:34.520 --> 00:21:36.880
in long terms, like, we're...

00:21:37.439 --> 00:21:40.159
Mostly we're using fat metabolism. You are so

00:21:40.159 --> 00:21:41.640
much more efficient than us.

00:21:41.880 --> 00:21:44.199
Yeah. And I think, too, our strategy for pacing

00:21:44.199 --> 00:21:46.760
potentially plays to a huge advantage.

00:21:47.760 --> 00:21:49.399
Because, yeah, I mean, I remember watching

00:21:49.399 --> 00:21:51.239
Kelsey... I don't remember her last name.

00:21:51.520 --> 00:21:52.199
But she was the woman who

00:21:52.199 --> 00:21:54.760
rowed from, like, L.A. to Hawaii.

00:21:54.880 --> 00:21:55.600
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:21:55.600 --> 00:21:57.960
And she blew that record out of the water. And

00:21:57.960 --> 00:21:58.680
it's like, "Yeah, just

00:21:58.680 --> 00:22:00.159
give us a long enough time."

00:22:00.159 --> 00:22:01.680
And we're probably actually gonna pay for it.

00:22:01.680 --> 00:22:03.520
Yeah, it was the Appalachian Trail. It was for...

00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:05.319
Tara; Via Alpina with Sophie.

00:22:06.359 --> 00:22:08.439
So, yeah, I mean, I definitely think that we

00:22:08.439 --> 00:22:10.000
don't necessarily have all of the

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:13.359
same tools at our disposal, but we...

00:22:13.840 --> 00:22:14.600
But these different tools.

00:22:14.960 --> 00:22:15.520
Yeah, exactly.

00:22:16.399 --> 00:22:18.159
The same as, like, you to

00:22:18.159 --> 00:22:19.840
Courtney, you have different tools.

00:22:20.079 --> 00:22:21.319
Me to another runner,

00:22:21.319 --> 00:22:22.039
like, we have different tools.

00:22:22.279 --> 00:22:25.159
So it's like how we use them to perform.

00:22:26.079 --> 00:22:28.199
But did you feel, like, on the races like this...

00:22:29.800 --> 00:22:32.159
Yeah. Pressure, like, of...

00:22:32.640 --> 00:22:33.680
Or that was during the race,

00:22:33.680 --> 00:22:35.399
you were, like, on your bubble?

00:22:35.640 --> 00:22:39.199
Or do you still feel it, like, it's the other

00:22:39.199 --> 00:22:41.119
man, when you pass a man, you're like, "Ah."

00:22:41.119 --> 00:22:41.680
You know, like, it's a lot of...

00:22:42.479 --> 00:22:44.760
I mean, Cocodona was not the first race that I'd

00:22:44.760 --> 00:22:47.039
won outright, and so I knew that it was possible.

00:22:47.239 --> 00:22:48.960
It was just, that was obviously the biggest

00:22:48.960 --> 00:22:50.319
stage, or the most

00:22:50.319 --> 00:22:52.159
competitive of the race, I suppose.

00:22:53.640 --> 00:22:56.760
I mean, I do very specifically remember the

00:22:56.760 --> 00:23:00.880
moment when I decided to leave Joe McConaughey,

00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:04.760
and just being like, "Okay, like, this is...

00:23:05.960 --> 00:23:07.520
Like, I think this is the move."

00:23:08.720 --> 00:23:10.640
Maybe, I mean, I didn't know if it was a move at

00:23:10.640 --> 00:23:12.880
the time, but I think that was it.

00:23:13.840 --> 00:23:16.399
And just kind of being like, "How stupid am I

00:23:16.399 --> 00:23:17.760
gonna feel when he catches me later?"

00:23:18.279 --> 00:23:21.640
And, like, I left at mile 60, and then I was

00:23:21.640 --> 00:23:22.399
like, "Wait a second,

00:23:22.399 --> 00:23:23.760
like, why am I thinking that?"

00:23:23.760 --> 00:23:24.560
Why are you thinking that?

00:23:24.560 --> 00:23:26.720
Because, I mean, I've improved at

00:23:26.720 --> 00:23:28.680
this race every year, and I, you know,

00:23:28.680 --> 00:23:30.720
I got second to Jimmy Elam at the Mammoth.

00:23:31.079 --> 00:23:32.640
So, like, obviously I'm...

00:23:32.640 --> 00:23:33.159
You're there.

00:23:33.159 --> 00:23:34.119
I know what I'm doing.

00:23:34.119 --> 00:23:34.840
You're coming to the capacity to get...

00:23:35.079 --> 00:23:36.520
Right, like, I know what I'm doing.

00:23:36.560 --> 00:23:38.720
And if this is what feels like the right thing to

00:23:38.720 --> 00:23:40.520
do, then why am I questioning that?

00:23:40.520 --> 00:23:43.199
Like, why can't I just be confident in this move?

00:23:43.199 --> 00:23:44.199
Because a man would be.

00:23:44.880 --> 00:23:45.680
And a man wouldn't think

00:23:45.680 --> 00:23:47.760
twice about taking the lead.

00:23:48.359 --> 00:23:49.439
So, I don't need to either.

00:23:50.600 --> 00:23:51.239
And, yeah, just...

00:23:51.800 --> 00:23:55.239
But there was a lot of moments late in the race

00:23:55.239 --> 00:23:57.399
when, you know, I'd been

00:23:57.399 --> 00:23:59.439
leading for so long that I...

00:24:00.600 --> 00:24:02.920
I thought it would be really cool if a woman won.

00:24:02.960 --> 00:24:05.560
And so, there was a lot of, like,

00:24:05.560 --> 00:24:06.880
"Oh, God, what happens if, like...

00:24:08.159 --> 00:24:09.720
What happens if that's not in the cards?"

00:24:10.159 --> 00:24:11.880
And, like, "What do I do?"

00:24:11.880 --> 00:24:13.520
And that was just kind of when I was like, "Nope.

00:24:13.760 --> 00:24:15.199
Like, we're not thinking that.

00:24:15.199 --> 00:24:16.840
We're just going to keep doing our best."

00:24:17.199 --> 00:24:18.399
And you can't worry about what

00:24:18.399 --> 00:24:19.640
kind of day Killian is having,

00:24:19.880 --> 00:24:21.720
and you can't worry about what kind of day Cody

00:24:21.720 --> 00:24:23.760
Poskin is having, or anybody else.

00:24:23.760 --> 00:24:26.520
Like, you just have to focus on your own day and

00:24:26.520 --> 00:24:27.800
just keep trying to move forward.

00:24:28.640 --> 00:24:29.680
And just keep eating your

00:24:29.680 --> 00:24:32.119
snacks, and drinking your stupid water,

00:24:32.159 --> 00:24:34.479
and doing all your salt crap, and whatever.

00:24:35.239 --> 00:24:36.520
But, like, at all costs.

00:24:36.520 --> 00:24:39.199
Like, you cannot control other people's days.

00:24:39.199 --> 00:24:40.159
You can only control your own.

00:24:41.359 --> 00:24:44.119
No, it was amazing to follow.

00:24:44.119 --> 00:24:46.600
And I think it was a moment that it will be,

00:24:46.600 --> 00:24:48.319
like, marked in the history of trail running,

00:24:48.319 --> 00:24:49.840
or, like, something changed.

00:24:50.199 --> 00:24:50.800
Maybe, yeah.

00:24:51.319 --> 00:24:54.359
And I hope that in two days,

00:24:54.359 --> 00:24:55.560
you have another day like that.

00:24:55.760 --> 00:24:57.800
That you have a nice race here

00:24:57.800 --> 00:24:59.119
around, and mostly, like, enjoy it.

00:24:59.800 --> 00:25:00.800
It's a wonderful race.

00:25:01.159 --> 00:25:07.199
It's an experience through the connection with

00:25:07.199 --> 00:25:09.199
those mountains, with the people that are here.

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:11.039
You enjoy a lot of that,

00:25:11.039 --> 00:25:12.920
and I wish you all the best.

00:25:13.159 --> 00:25:13.659
Thank you.

00:25:14.000 --> 00:25:15.000
Do you have any advice for me?

00:25:17.039 --> 00:25:18.279
I feel like now is the time.

00:25:18.640 --> 00:25:21.680
The night, I saw the weather forecast.

00:25:21.920 --> 00:25:22.680
Like, it's a race that I

00:25:22.680 --> 00:25:24.520
think the night is very hard.

00:25:26.079 --> 00:25:29.520
Not the night itself, but on how the night goes.

00:25:29.520 --> 00:25:32.159
But then the day, it's very different.

00:25:33.119 --> 00:25:34.199
Because sometimes you push

00:25:34.199 --> 00:25:35.640
through the night and you feel good,

00:25:35.640 --> 00:25:37.359
but then, like, in the day, it's really like a...

00:25:37.920 --> 00:25:39.079
You feel like a hammer.

00:25:39.840 --> 00:25:40.800
So I think, like, the night

00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:44.359
here, it's important to save energy.

00:25:44.640 --> 00:25:46.560
Okay.
Even if you go fast, like, you need to go fast.

00:25:46.560 --> 00:25:47.640
You need to try to...

00:25:47.640 --> 00:25:48.720
Or, like, you know where, like...

00:25:48.720 --> 00:25:49.220
Right.

00:25:49.399 --> 00:25:50.159
But...
Still a race.

00:25:50.159 --> 00:25:50.760
Yeah, still a race.

00:25:51.279 --> 00:25:53.680
But I mean, like, sometimes you feel like...

00:25:54.119 --> 00:25:55.760
Especially the start is fast.

00:25:56.159 --> 00:25:58.640
And it's so many people that you feel like,

00:25:58.880 --> 00:26:00.600
"Oh, I'm losing terrain," or that.

00:26:00.600 --> 00:26:03.159
And it's like, no, like, if you need to take...

00:26:03.159 --> 00:26:05.039
If it's bad weather and you need to take time to

00:26:05.039 --> 00:26:09.039
put the rain jacket, do it.

00:26:09.039 --> 00:26:11.359
If you need to put, like, gloves, do it, like...

00:26:12.319 --> 00:26:14.000
To lose 30 seconds, one

00:26:14.000 --> 00:26:15.960
minute here, it's worth it.

00:26:15.960 --> 00:26:17.000
Because if not during the

00:26:17.000 --> 00:26:18.920
next day, it really changes.

00:26:19.279 --> 00:26:19.779
Okay.

00:26:20.279 --> 00:26:20.779
Yeah.

00:26:20.800 --> 00:26:21.300
I am...

00:26:21.439 --> 00:26:23.000
The biggest part that I'm

00:26:23.000 --> 00:26:24.079
concerned about is the start.

00:26:24.439 --> 00:26:25.720
Because I do think that it will be very fast.

00:26:25.760 --> 00:26:27.199
It's fast, yeah.

00:26:27.760 --> 00:26:29.760
The first 30k, it's like people is racing.

00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:32.119
Sometimes you are there, "Wow, we

00:26:32.119 --> 00:26:33.880
are a group of, like, 30 people here.

00:26:34.159 --> 00:26:35.039
What's going on?"

00:26:35.039 --> 00:26:37.239
And then it's like, suddenly, like, you are five.

00:26:38.079 --> 00:26:40.800
So the first 30k thing is, like, need to pass

00:26:40.800 --> 00:26:41.840
through those kilometers.

00:26:42.199 --> 00:26:42.800
I think so, too.

00:26:43.119 --> 00:26:44.479
Needs to pass through there, and then just...

00:26:45.319 --> 00:26:45.819
Yeah.

00:26:46.079 --> 00:26:47.520
And then the night is just like, okay, it's

00:26:47.520 --> 00:26:49.880
mostly that because it's...

00:26:50.720 --> 00:26:52.279
People try to go really, really fast.

00:26:52.560 --> 00:26:54.960
And you see someone, like, pushing, running down

00:26:54.960 --> 00:26:56.319
into Courmayeur, like,

00:26:57.000 --> 00:26:58.039
you don't want to lose that

00:26:58.039 --> 00:26:59.960
ground, and it's still like a long race.

00:27:00.279 --> 00:27:00.779
Yeah.

00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:01.500
Okay.

00:27:01.640 --> 00:27:02.279
That's good to know.

00:27:02.279 --> 00:27:02.920
I'll lock it away.

00:27:03.399 --> 00:27:04.520
No, you will have an amazing race.

00:27:05.119 --> 00:27:05.619
Thank you.

00:27:06.720 --> 00:27:07.079
Cool.

00:27:07.079 --> 00:27:07.880
Thank you very much.

00:27:07.880 --> 00:27:08.640
Yeah, thank you.

00:27:08.640 --> 00:27:10.199
No, no, it's a pleasure.

